{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/the-crossroads-continent/1538541-current-campaign-updates-and-notes","id":1538541,"title":"Current Campaign Updates and Notes","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","The Crossroads Continent"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-07-13T14:25:08-07:00","id":1538541,"title":"Current Campaign Updates and Notes","content":"Noticed that the original thread for this was really meant to be about starting this new campaign, and I intended to start a new thread for write-ups and notes for the campaign itself. But, I forgot and just added to the original thread. So we're going to fix that and give the actual campaign the thread it deserves! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> After the first session the gang rolled up characters and did a short getting-together session, and we now have:<br /> <br /> <br /> - J'imknee. A Mantid Bard. 6\u2019 green shiny chitin. Kicked out of the hive for rockin\u2019 too hard and now wandering from performance to performance. He also makes frothy mead and sells it.<br /> <br /> - Corsika C. M'udd. A Sea Elf Fighter (pirate). 6.5\u2019 tall. Her ship was sunk by the Imperial (Empire of the Scarlet Gate) Navy and rowed a small dinghy with a few other survivors to the port at the Hill of the First Horse. Two weapon fighter.<br /> <br /> - Dampe D. M'udd. Her Brother, a Sea Elf Ranger. 6'6&quot; tall. Intends to be a pirate admiral and possibly overthrow the current admiral, Austin Mearic, aka The Red Grin, an infamous villain and member of the Order of the Black Cross. Archer and known for his acrobatic flourishes.<br /> <br /> - Hugh Vandele. A Human Cleric of the Norse sea god. Bald with nice clothes. Nature and Tempest domains. A lesser noble booted out by his dad as he\u2019s the seventh son and kind of useless. But now he has found religion and headed south to the Hill of the First Horse.<br /> <br /> - SmillBjorg. A Dwarven Wizard who had been captured by the same sunken pirate vessel that the Sea Elves (who he escaped with) had been crew for. Looking for more magical knowledge and artifacts to bring back to the very small wizard enclave he had been a part of.<br /> <br /> The three are sharing a table in one of the bars (Pappy\u2019s) at the docks at the Port of the City of the Hill of the First Horse. The dock guards have already popped in and scoped out the trio, but backed off without questions after seeing the noble\u2019s traveling papers. They are joined outside as they begin to explore the port town by:<br /> <br /> - Alabaster. A Winged One (Elf) Druid who worships his own idea of a god of the air and sky - whom he loudly and incessantly proselytizes for. Is wary and nervous when not flying.<br /> <br /> Various errands are run. The lesser noble pays for much of what they buy in preparation for heading north, out of the Kingdom of Twenty Horses, to a land the lesser noble has heard of where worship of his god is more known. The ground slopes up from the docks, and in the flatter areas more to the southeast of the hill itself is where the actual town lies, with the higher ground more of a citadel. In that town is where the tradesmen are, stables and furthest out, before the ground begins dropping again, are tanners, blacksmiths, and stables. While in town, in a tavern, J'imknee begins plying his musical trade, earning the party some free drinks and tips. Which are unfortunately snatched, the coins that is, and the group pursues the thief through several back alleys before losing him. Even the Ranger can find no clue, until Alabaster, who was flying above unwilling to enter the confining tavern, let's the rest know that he saw the thief duck into a hidden door disguised by a pile of crates in an alley.<br /> <br /> Moving the crates aside is a surprisingly easy thing to - they must be rigged with wheels and pulleys suggests the wizard - revealing stairs down into a tunnel. What to do, what to do...<br /> <br /> Down into the tunnels the party goes, although Alabaster, the Winged one, only goes a few feet in before freaking out by the closeness of the tunnel, and, wings flapping against the walls, he scrambles out of the hidden opening and, shuddering, takes to the night skies. Unshaken by his sudden panic, the rest of the party continues down the corridor. The walls are dressed stonework, with sconces for torches randomly situated every twenty feet or so. It's dingy, with small bit of debris strewn about and dried mud staining the floors. Clearly, this is a passageway that sees some traffic, although the party can only be sure that the thief they're chasing used it recently. Hugh casts a small spell that provides some light for him, although the others don't need it. But considering that no one in the party has any real training in the thiefly arts, every pair of eyes that can be used to note possible traps and secret doors has real value. Even the wizard SmillBjorg's years of hearing lectures from his stonemason relatives comes in handy as the dwarf notes the high quality of the stonework, as well as it being several centuries old, although not quite up to dwarven standards. This city on the Hill of the First Horse has been here for quite a long time.<br /> <br /> Although they find neither traps not hidden panels before descending a staircase to a lower level of passages, they do find that there are very thin wires stretched across their current hallway whose ends go into small holes in the walls, and SmillBjorg notes that not only did they descend the staircase to this passage, but the passage itself is subtly slanting down towards an intersection. The tunnel T's at the end, with two massive ten foot wide iron-banded wooden doors with pull rings at the base of the passage they were following, and a two passages leading off in opposite directions. It is while they are slowly moving forward watching those passages and the big doors that the the trapdoor in the floor gives way!<br /> <br /> Meanwhile, Alabaster is quietly circling the area high above in the darkness and sees several groups of ruffians meet up and travel to another hidden door in the ground down an alley. They have lit the torches they brought with them, and brandishing a variety of weapons, the gang, numbering about twenty, descends into the earth. Having circled lower Alabaster can catch enough of the low muttered conversation of the angry throng to realize that they are headed down into the gang's base in order to deal with &quot;the trespassers&quot;. The Winged One may often seem deluded as he rants about his god, The Sky, but he doesn't hesitate to follow them underground in hopes of assisting the party of adventurers he has only just met.<br /> <br /> Meanwhile meanwhile, as the five foot long and ten foot wide section of flooring pivots beneath their feet, the Ranger dives forward and rolls to his feet on the other side. Good thing, as his sister, who has upgraded her armor since their time at sea, slips in her metal boots as she tries to jump and barely manages to clutch the far edge of the pit and needs his hands up as an assist to get to safety. The bug leaps across easily with his Mantid insect-like lower legs, and then throws a rope to the cleric, who then doesn't need the safety measure as he broad jumps the five foot gap. The cleric, our lesser noble Hugh Vandele, is also the only one who looks down into the ten foot deep pit to note the web of thin wires mid-way down, just below where the trick flooring section swung down. While dirty, there seems to be no spikes or bones or anything suggesting the trap was designed to be more than a delaying nuisance and some sort of alarm. The others split up to listen at the door and the branching tunnels. It's the Sea Elf fighter who let's the party know that she hears what sounds like a crowd approaching as stealthily as they can from the left hand passage.<br /> <br /> &quot;Yeah, time to fight!&quot; she hisses, drawing a sword for each hand and trotting up the passage, closely followed by her brother and J'imknee, the bug bard, who chitters something about getting their money back. Hugh and the dwarf follow more slowly; one because of being cautious and the other because of his short legs. This passage slants slightly upwards to a flight of ten stairs across the width of the passage, and the party can clearly catch the wavering glow of torchlight coming towards them as they surmount the stairs. They're about twenty feet away from what seems like quite a large group in the shifting light, and a roar goes out as that gang realizes that the &quot;trespassers&quot; are not only in front of them, but are charging to meet them! The gang lurches forward and even manage to fire some ineffective arrows.<br /> <br /> Just as the Sea Elf, Corsika, cuts down one of the gang with two slashes even before he can react, a terrifying apparition appears behind the street toughs: mighty wings flapping wildly, shrieking incoherently, Alabaster fills the hall and then hugging tight to the ceiling, he soars over their heads and turning casts a spell creating a thunderous wave of force, throwing most of the street gang off their feet, buffeted and rolling down the hall into, and in some cases past, their friends (along with sprays of blood and other body fluids as some of the gang are crushed by the impact). The thunder clap from this spell rolls through the tunnel, causing much wincing and even fear from friend and foe alike! The gang tries to clump together and still attack, and some successfully get through the party's defenses, but the Ranger does a diving roll and impales a tough on his spear, his sister double-hacks another of their tougher toughs down, Hugh smashes another with his mace, and the bug strums his arms together while blurting out a cruel insult at a heavily built gang member, causing the ganger to drop to his knees, his morale broken. At this point, now having crested the stairs, and with ears ringing from the thunder, the dwarf wizard fires off three bolts of mystic energy that drop two more of the gang - this is more than the street toughs bargained for, and even though there are still some tougher thieves in the remaining gang, they break and rush back down the hall. All except for the one on his knees who sadly puts his hands up like he's ready to be shackled.<br /> <br /> The Ranger and self-styled Captain for the &quot;crew&quot;, Dampe, won't have this, though, and encourages the young street tough, who mumbles that his name is Malcolm, to join their crew! Dampe just knows that they'll have a ship soon, or at least a boat, and Malcolm can join them working on the water with the sun and the wind and the bracing salt spray - which has got to be better than a life in the dirty city streets thieving! Malcolm interjects that he was never really a thief in the gang so much as an errand boy and shifter of boxes that the real thieves had stolen. After some aggressive interrogation by Hugh, who just doesn't like poor Malcolm, the young tough lets the team know that this whole underground complex is where the real thieves and smugglers hide out. He doesn't know where those thieves are, who he still speaks of with admiration, but while he never was allowed inside the big wooden doors, he knows that behind the doors is the main warehouse and resting place and a key part of their smuggling operation.<br /> <br /> And with that, the party has to decide what to do next...\u200b<br /> <br /> Thanks!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5-richt","name":"RichT","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5_2.jpeg","title":"Onyx Path Creative Director","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"505"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-25T13:28:46-07:00","id":1539240,"title":"","content":"Some DM notes: To pull back the curtain a bit, this was very much a improvised session on my part in that I knew that I wanted to give the group a chance to get used to two things: exploration and combat. For the first one, a few corridors, some hidden panels and traps, and some &quot;what do we explore next?&quot; moments, got the group started. Finding and avoiding some traps and an ongoing series of perception rolls built the suspense, while active precautions paying off built up the feeling that the world had cause and effect and that there was a logical progression to things. The combat section is just something a gaming group needs to run through a few times until the teamwork starts happening, which gives everybody more of a feeling as to why these adventurers troublemakers are all together.<br /> <br /> So, having them run into a snatch 'n' grab by a street tough, to the tough's street gang and just a couple of actual brigands/thieves, and leave them ready to enter the harder test of fighting actual thieves and smugglers behind the big doors, meant that the tunnels needed to be trapped but not lethally, which I meta-decided was because it was actually a reasonably active way the smugglers transported their ill-booten gooty away from official eyes and so they didn't want to be shutting off or dismantling their complicated death traps every time a shipment came through.<br /> <br /> A note on the combat section and what happened with Malcolm the street gang member. This was excellently played out by the players, as when the bug used the bard cantrip Vicious Mockery for the first time, and with the bug being their first character, they were completely caught by surprise when I told them to sing it out - to actually roleplay what they said and act it out loud. So they made a crack about the NPCs weight. Which was so entirely out of character for the player, who is completely non judgemental and is just always so supportive that the whole table were stunned. If this had been a problem for anyone at the table, we had already established that verbally stopping things was OK, as was slipping me a note.<br /> <br /> Literally rolling with it, I rolled two six-siders as &quot;morale dice&quot;, which I like to use for situations where stress is coming down on the NPCs. I rolled snake-eyes, and poor Malcolm was crushed by the cruel barb as he had been working out because the gang had also been teasing him. Suddenly, this game mechanic moment was now a character moment, and the players, by-and-large, were super-sympathetic to Malcolm. At this point, the player of the sea-elf Ranger, Dampe, who had been modeling his character on a certain optimistic and positive aspiring sea captain from a certain Anime, successfully jumped in with an inspirational speech that turned Malcolm into a loyal member of his crew. I didn't roll any dice for that as it was so well timed and appropriate to the characters and the direction they wanted the story to go in.<br /> <br /> Next session, though, I prepared a map and notes on the NPCs and their activities depending on what the players did - in the broadest of terms since I didn't really know just what they were capable of - so that the players could get used to using one for tactical situations like battles - and so they'd have a stronger feel for the environs they found themselves in. That adds into whatever level of description I provide to encourage the players to visualize the setting, which I <i>hope </i>they find cool and interesting, and their character's actions in that space. We'll find out next session!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5-richt","name":"RichT","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5_2.jpeg","title":"Onyx Path Creative Director","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"505"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-28T11:50:19-07:00","id":1539436,"title":"","content":"that all sounds very cool!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4621-nicolas-milioni","name":"Nicolas Milioni","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4621_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3605"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}